<title>DENMARK: Danish, Swedish beet tests show mixed yields.</title>
<headline>Danish, Swedish beet tests show mixed yields.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1996-09-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>Results of Denmark's third beet tests conducted on September 23 and issued by sole producer Danisco A/S on Wednesday showed 8.26 tonnes of sugar per hectare vs an 8.42 percent average over the past five years with sugar content at 18.0 percent (16.1).</p>
<p>Figures for Sweden were 7.99 (7.81) tonnes/hectare and sugar content 18.1 percent (16.0).</p>
<p>A table with year-ago comparisons follows-  </p>
<p>DENMARK</p>
<p>			3rd test 96   3rd test 95  2nd test 96</p>
<p> Tonnes beet per hectare     45.9	   51.4	     39.3</p>
<p> Percent sugar in beet	 18.0	   15.5	     15.7</p>
<p> Tonnes sugar per hectare    8.26	   7.94	     6.18</p>
<p>SWEDEN</p>
<p> Tonnes beet per hectare     44.1	   51.0		38.0</p>
<p> Percent sugar in beet	 18.1	   15.9		15.7</p>
<p> Tonnes sugar per hectare    7.99	   8.10		5.95</p>
<p>In eastern Germany, where Danisco has one factory, the figures were as follows-</p>
<p>    EASTERN GERMANY	3rd test 96   3rd test 95   2nd test 96</p>
<p> Tonnes beet per hectare  55.9		49.2	   43.6</p>
<p> Percent sugar in beet    16.9		15.4	   14.8</p>
<p> Tonnes sugar per hectare 9.46		7.57	   6.44</p>
<p>Danisco, with a virtual monopoly of production in Denmark and Sweden as well as its operation in Germany, is Europe's fourth biggest sugar producer. It said it expected overall 1996 sugar production to be the same or better than 1995.</p>
<p>--Chris Follett, Copenhagen newsroom +45-33969652</p>
